ips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:\nLet me analyze this situation carefully.\n\n**Current Situation:**\n- My hand: 4♦ J♥\n- Board: Q♣ 8♣ 10♦ 9♣\n- This is the Turn (Round 2), one more card to come\n- Opponent raised 259 chips\n\n**Hand Analysis:**\nI have J-high with a gutshot straight draw. I need a King to complete a straight (K-Q-J-10-9). That's 4 outs (four Kings in the deck).\n\n**Pot Odds Calculation:**\n- Opponent bet 259\n- To call, I need 259 chips\n- Pot after opponent's bet: approximately 518 (assuming small bets earlier)\n- I'm getting roughly 2:1 pot odds\n\n**Winning Probability:**\n- With 4 outs and 1 card to come: roughly 4/46 = 8.7% chance to hit the straight\n- Currently, I only have J-high, which is very weak\n- Even if I hit the King, opponent could have a flush (4 clubs on board) or already have a straight\n\n**Risk Assessment:**\n- The board shows Q♣ 8♣ 10♦ 9♣ - three clubs, meaning a flush is very possible\n- Opponent raised on the Turn, suggesting strength\n- If I hit my King, any club gives opponent a flush\n- Even without the flush threat, opponent could already have a straight (any King-Jack, Jack-7, or 7-6)\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- I'm ahead in cumulative chips (+2423)\n- Calling 259 to chase a 4-outer with flush possibilities on board is not profitable\n- The pot odds (2:1) don't justify calling with 8.7% equity, especially with the flush threat\n- Raising would be a bluff with minimal fold equity given opponent's Turn aggression\n\nReason:\nThis is a clear fold. I have only Jack-high with a weak gutshot straight draw (4 outs, ~8.7% to hit). The pot odds of roughly 2:1 don't justify calling with such low equity. More importantly, even if I hit my King, three clubs on board means any opponent holding clubs likely has a flush, which beats my straight. The opponent's Turn raise indicates strength, and I'm drawing thin against any reasonable hand. With a comfortable chip lead overall, there's no reason to gamble in this marginal spot.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
